---
// BlogPostArticle.astro — the reading-focused post shell: a `phthalo` header
// band (breadcrumb, meta line, title, standfirst), the rendered Markdown body
// (styled by hand below — no @tailwindcss/typography, per this build's own
// hard constraint), the post's topics, a quiet CTA, and a prev/next/back-to-
// index footer. `<slot />` carries the post's rendered `<Content />` — passed
// as a child from the calling page, since an Astro component factory (the
// return of `render()`) can only be composed as markup at the call site, not
// threaded through as a plain prop.
//
// ---- 2026-09-01 redesign (USER REPORT) ----
// The owner rejected the shipped post page on a laptop. Four defects, all
// reproduced at 1440×900 before this rewrite, and what each one is now:
//
//   1. The reading column was `max-w-measure` (56ch) START-ALIGNED inside the
//      1180px content wrapper, so the whole inline-end half of a 1440 screen
//      was dead space. It is now `mx-auto max-w-measure-prose` — centred, and
//      widened to 64ch, which renders around 70 typeset characters per line
//      (inside the 50–75 band every readability source names).
//   2. In-article H2s used `text-h2`, the site's MARKETING section clamp —
//      44px at 1440, against a 44px H1. There was no hierarchy left inside an
//      article at all. H2/H3/body/lede now use the four blog-scoped fontSize
//      tokens added to tailwind.config.mjs in the same change (26/19/18/20px
//      at 1440 against a 44px title), so a section break reads as subordinate
//      to the title instead of competing with it.
//   3. A large awkward gap sat between the tag chips and the first paragraph
//      — the header section's own bottom padding, the next section's
//      `py-section-tight`, and the first paragraph's margin all stacking.
//      Both sections now carry explicit `padding-block` pairs, and
//      the first block of the article has its own block-start margin zeroed.
//   4. The tags read raw and unfinished: bordered mono chips wedged between
//      the title and the body, i.e. UI furniture interrupting the read. They
//      are now plain subdued text in the article's own footer, where a
//      reader who has finished the piece is actually looking for them.
//
// `phthalo` (#123524, the owner's 2026-09-01 "our brand green" directive) is
// the blog's one new surface — the header band here and the closing CTA
// panel, matching BlogIndex.astro so index and post read as one room. The
// running text stays on `ink`: a long read wants the site's darkest, flattest
// ground behind it, not a tinted panel.
//
// CAUTION, found while measuring this redesign in the browser: `body` is BOTH
// a `fontSize` key and a `colors` key in tailwind.config.mjs, so `text-body`
// emits a font-size rule as well as a colour rule. Tailwind orders the
// generated fontSize utilities alphabetically, which puts `.text-body` after
// every `.text-blog-*` — so a `class="text-blog-lede … text-body"` element
// silently renders at 1rem, not at the blog token. Measured, not assumed: the
// standfirst below rendered at 16px instead of 20px until the colour utility
// was dropped. Anything that needs a blog size AND the body colour must let
// the colour inherit (global.css applies it to the `body` element) rather than
// restate it. Logged in known-issues.md.
//
// No `.tm-rise`/reveal script here either — see BlogIndex.astro's own header
// comment for why the blog section skips the site's scroll-reveal mechanism
// outright rather than gating it per element. Nothing here transforms, so
// there is no `prefers-reduced-motion` branch to write: every state is a
// colour transition, which is information rather than motion.
import Section from "../layout/Section.astro";
import Button from "../ui/Button.astro";
import { useTranslations } from "../../i18n/utils";
import type { Language } from "../../i18n/types";
import {
  formatPostDate,
  formatReadingTime,
  readingTime,
  type BlogPost,
} from "../../lib/blog";
import tailwindConfig from "../../../tailwind.config.mjs";

interface Props {
  lang: Language;
  post: BlogPost;
  prevPost: BlogPost | null;
  nextPost: BlogPost | null;
  /** The post's own h2 headings, already filtered to depth 2 by the calling
   * route, straight out of `render(post)` — `slug` is by construction the
   * `id` the Markdown pipeline wrote onto that heading, so a rail link cannot
   * point at an anchor that is not there. An empty array is a valid state
   * (a post with no sections): the rail then renders nothing at all rather
   * than an empty labelled landmark. */
  headings: { depth: number; slug: string; text: string }[];
}

const { lang, post, prevPost, nextPost, headings }: Props = Astro.props;
const t = useTranslations(lang);

const minutes = readingTime(post.body ?? "", lang);
const readLabel = formatReadingTime(minutes, lang, {
  few: t("blog.readingTimeFew"),
  other: t("blog.readingTimeOther"),
});

// The rendered Markdown body arrives through `<slot />` from another
// component tree entirely (the post's `<Content />`), so the `.blog-prose
// :global(...)` rules below can't reach it via Tailwind utility classes —
// only via literal CSS. To keep every size/colour a token read from
// tailwind.config.mjs rather than a second hardcoded copy (that file's own
// header: "every colour, duration, ease and measurement... is a token
// defined here"), the values are read from the config in JS here and handed
// to the `<style>` block as CSS custom properties via `define:vars` — the
// same "read tailwindConfig at build time" approach ProductHero.astro/
// AiMomentCard.astro already use for values Tailwind's own utility classes
// can't express, just handed to CSS through custom properties instead of an
// inline `style` string, since these rules must reach slotted content.
//
// The four size entries below are the BLOG-scoped tokens, not the site's
// `h2`/`h3`/`body`/`lede` — see this file's header comment, defect 2. Each
// fontSize token is `[size, { lineHeight, letterSpacing }]`, and unlike a
// Tailwind `text-*` utility (which emits all three), a bare `font-size` in
// hand-written CSS brings neither companion value with it. So the
// line-heights are pulled out and declared explicitly too; skipping that is
// exactly how the old rules ended up inheriting the body's 1.55 onto a 44px
// heading.
const theme = (
  tailwindConfig as unknown as {
    theme: {
      colors: Record<string, string>;
      fontFamily: { sans: string[] };
      fontWeight: Record<string, string>;
      fontSize: Record<string, [string, Record<string, string>]>;
      lineHeight: Record<string, string>;
      letterSpacing: Record<string, string>;
      extend: {
        borderWidth: Record<string, string>;
        spacing: Record<string, string>;
      };
    };
  }
).theme;

// ---- 2026-09-01 wide-screen pass (second USER REPORT: "blogs' content is
// not increasing width wise on large screens, at least it should stretch till
// width of nav bar content … lot of empty space both sides in big monitor
// screen"). ----
//
// The fix is a composition, not a wider paragraph. Inflating the single
// column to the full 1036px content box would have put ~115 characters on a
// line, which trades a layout complaint for a worse reading one. Instead the
// page becomes a two-track grid at `lg` that spans the whole content box: a
// sticky table-of-contents rail in track one, the article in track two, and
// the three widths (16.5rem + 4.25rem + 44rem) sum to exactly the 1036px the
// header's own `max-w-content`/`px-gutter` wrapper gives its brand mark and
// CTA. The article column itself grew 576px → 704px and the body type grew
// with it, so characters-per-line stayed inside the readable band.
//
// These two class strings are declared once and reused by the three blocks
// that have to line up on that grid — the article body, the topics footer and
// the closing CTA/nav. Written out at each call site instead, three copies
// would silently drift the moment one of them changed. The header band is
// deliberately NOT one of them: it spans the whole content box on the owner's
// own instruction, so its start edge matches the rail's rather than the
// article column's. See its own comment in the template below.
//
// `col-start-2` and `grid-cols-*` are direction-agnostic: CSS grid places
// tracks along the INLINE axis in writing order, so track one is the start
// side in both languages and the rail mirrors to the other edge under
// `dir="rtl"` with no override. Same reason `border-s`/`ps-*` are used inside
// the rail rather than a physical pair.
// `lg:w-full` is load-bearing, not belt-and-braces. A grid item only STRETCHES
// to its track when its inline size is `auto` AND neither inline margin is
// `auto` — and `mx-auto` (needed for the centred single column below `lg`)
// sets both. So without an explicit width these blocks shrink to fit their own
// content and centre inside the track: measured 540px for the topics footer
// and 543px for the CTA panel against a 704px column, while the prose itself
// happened to look correct only because a paragraph's max-content width
// exceeds the cap anyway. `w-full` gives a definite inline size, the auto
// margins resolve to 0, and all four blocks land on the same 704px column.
const gridClass = "lg:grid lg:grid-cols-blog lg:gap-x-blog-gap";
const columnClass =
  "mx-auto max-w-measure-prose lg:col-start-2 lg:w-full lg:max-w-measure-prose-wide";

const proseVars = {
  proseFontSans: theme.fontFamily.sans.join(", "),
  proseWeightSemi: theme.fontWeight.semi,
  proseH2Size: theme.fontSize["blog-h2"][0],
  proseH2Leading: theme.fontSize["blog-h2"][1].lineHeight,
  proseH2Tracking: theme.fontSize["blog-h2"][1].letterSpacing,
  proseH3Size: theme.fontSize["blog-h3"][0],
  proseH3Leading: theme.fontSize["blog-h3"][1].lineHeight,
  proseH3Tracking: theme.fontSize["blog-h3"][1].letterSpacing,
  proseBodySize: theme.fontSize["blog-body"][0],
  proseLedeSize: theme.fontSize["blog-lede"][0],
  proseLineHeightLatin: theme.lineHeight.prose,
  proseLineHeightArabic: theme.lineHeight["prose-ar"],
  proseLineHeadingArabic: theme.lineHeight["heading-ar"],
  proseLetterSpacingNone: theme.letterSpacing.none,
  proseColorPaper: theme.colors.paper,
  proseColorBody: theme.colors.body,
  proseColorAccent: theme.colors.accent,
  proseColorHairline: theme.colors.hairline,
  proseMarkerWidth: theme.extend.borderWidth.marker,
  // Anchor landing offset for the TOC's own links — see the `scroll-margin`
  // declaration in the style block below.
  proseAnchorOffset: theme.extend.spacing["sticky-top"],
};
---

{/* One article element per post, wrapping the header band and the body: the
    two together are the self-contained, syndicatable thing the element means,
    so the H1 has to be inside it, and it therefore has to sit outside both
    Sections rather than inside either. The CTA and the prev/next nav are
    deliberately left outside it — neither is part of the post. Section
    renders a section element, and an article containing sections is valid
    HTML, so this adds no generic wrapper; it names a region that was
    previously unnamed (the built pages carried zero article elements, which
    is what the SEO pass flagged). */}
<article>
  <Section
    pad={false}
    class="border-b border-hairline"
    style="padding-block: clamp(2.25rem, 6vh, 3.75rem) clamp(1.75rem, 4.5vh, 2.75rem);"
  >
    <div slot="backdrop" class="absolute inset-0 bg-phthalo"></div>

    {/* The header band is the one block that is NOT on the article grid
        (owner, 2026-09-01, after seeing the first wide-screen build: "use this
        heading in the wide format to match the width of 'On this page'
        section"). It runs the full content box, so the eyebrow, H1, standfirst
        and byline all start on the same grid line the table-of-contents rail
        starts on rather than on the article column's line — the masthead sets
        the page's full width, and the rail plus column composition begins
        underneath it. `Section` already supplies that box
        (`mx-auto max-w-content px-gutter`), so this needs no wrapper class of
        its own; the plain div is here only to keep the four blocks grouped. */}
    <div>
      <div>
        <a
          href={`/${lang}/blog/`}
          class="font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted transition-colors duration-micro ease-micro hover:text-accent"
        >
          {t("nav.blog")}
        </a>

        <h1
          class="mt-5 font-sans text-blog-title font-semi text-paper"
          style="text-wrap: pretty;"
        >
          {post.data.title}
        </h1>

        {/* The standfirst keeps a bounded measure while still STARTING on the
            band's own start edge — a 1036px line of 21.6px text would be
            ~104 characters, which is exactly the reading defect the article
            column below exists to avoid. `max-w-*` with no `mx-auto` caps the
            width without moving the block, so it grows from the same grid
            line as the H1 above it.

            No `text-body` colour utility here, deliberately — see this file's
            header note on the fontSize/colour name collision. The colour is
            inherited: global.css's base layer applies `text-body` to the
            `body` element itself, so every descendant already renders in that
            colour unless something overrides it. */}
        <p class="mt-5 max-w-measure-prose-wide text-blog-lede font-light">
          {post.data.description}
        </p>

        <p
          class="mt-6 font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted"
        >
          <time datetime={post.data.pubDate.toISOString()}>
            {formatPostDate(post.data.pubDate, lang)}
          </time>
          {" · "}{post.data.author}{" · "}{readLabel}
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section pad={false} style="padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 4.5vh, 2.75rem) 0;">
    <div class={gridClass}>
      {
        headings.length > 0 && (
          /* The rail. `lg:self-start` is what makes `lg:sticky` work here: a
             stretched grid item is already as tall as its area, so it has
             nowhere to stick to — shrunk to its own height, it sticks inside
             the area instead, and because the article body and this nav are
             pinned to the SAME grid row, the rail travels with the prose and
             stops at the topics footer rather than following the reader into
             the closing CTA.

             `hidden lg:block`: on a phone the rail would be a second copy of
             the article's own headings pushed above the first paragraph —
             clutter, and it costs a reader the opening of the piece. The
             headings themselves are still in the document either way.

             No JavaScript, and no scroll-behaviour of any kind: these are
             plain in-page anchors, so they work with scripting off and the
             jump is instant, which is already the correct behaviour under
             `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`. Nothing here needs a
             reduced-motion branch because nothing here animates. */
          <nav
            aria-labelledby="blog-toc-label"
            class="hidden lg:sticky lg:top-sticky-top lg:col-start-1 lg:row-start-1 lg:block lg:max-h-toc lg:self-start lg:overflow-y-auto"
          >
            <p
              id="blog-toc-label"
              class="font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted"
            >
              {t("blog.tocLabel")}
            </p>
            <ol class="mt-4 flex flex-col gap-3 border-s border-hairline">
              {headings.map((heading) => (
                <li>
                  <a
                    href={`#${heading.slug}`}
                    class="block ps-4 text-small font-light text-muted transition-colors duration-micro ease-micro hover:text-paper"
                  >
                    {heading.text}
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          </nav>
        )
      }

      <div class:list={["blog-prose", columnClass, "lg:row-start-1"]}>
        <slot />
      </div>

      <footer class:list={[columnClass, "mt-12 border-t border-hairline pt-8"]}>
        <p
          id="blog-topics-label"
          class="font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted"
        >
          {t("blog.tagsLabel")}
        </p>
        <ul
          aria-labelledby="blog-topics-label"
          class="tag-list mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center font-mono text-datum font-data tracking-datum text-muted"
        >
          {post.data.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
        </ul>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </Section>
</article>

<Section
  pad={false}
  style="padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vh, 3.5rem) clamp(3rem, 8vh, 5.5rem);"
>
  <div class={gridClass}>
    <aside
      class:list={[
        columnClass,
        "rounded-panel border border-hairline bg-phthalo",
      ]}
      style="padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);"
    >
      <h2 class="font-sans text-blog-h2 font-semi text-paper">
        {t("blog.ctaHeading")}
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-small font-light text-body" style="max-width: 52ch;">
        {t("blog.ctaBody")}
      </p>
      <Button href={`/${lang}/contact/`} class="mt-6">{t("cta.primary")}</Button>
    </aside>

    <nav
      aria-label={t("blog.indexLabel")}
      class:list={[
        columnClass,
        "mt-12 flex flex-col gap-6 border-t border-hairline pt-8",
      ]}
    >
      <a
        href={`/${lang}/blog/`}
        class="text-small font-semi text-accent transition-colors duration-micro ease-micro hover:text-paper"
      >
        {t("blog.indexLabel")}
      </a>
      {
        (prevPost || nextPost) && (
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-start justify-between gap-6">
            <div>
              {prevPost && (
                <a
                  href={`/${lang}/blog/${prevPost.data.translationKey}/`}
                  class="group block"
                >
                  <p class="font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted">
                    {t("blog.previousLabel")}
                  </p>
                  <p class="mt-2 text-small font-semi text-paper transition-colors duration-micro ease-micro group-hover:text-accent">
                    {prevPost.data.title}
                  </p>
                </a>
              )}
            </div>
            <div class="text-end">
              {nextPost && (
                <a
                  href={`/${lang}/blog/${nextPost.data.translationKey}/`}
                  class="group block"
                >
                  <p class="font-mono text-datum font-data uppercase tracking-datum text-muted">
                    {t("blog.nextLabel")}
                  </p>
                  <p class="mt-2 text-small font-semi text-paper transition-colors duration-micro ease-micro group-hover:text-accent">
                    {nextPost.data.title}
                  </p>
                </a>
              )}
            </div>
          </div>
        )
      }
    </nav>
  </div>
</Section>

<style define:vars={proseVars}>
  /* Markdown styled by hand (CLAUDE.md: no new dependencies — no
   * @tailwindcss/typography). `:global()` because the elements below come
   * from the rendered Markdown Content component passed through `<slot />`,
   * not written literally in this component's own template — Astro's
   * per-component style scoping only reaches markup authored in the file
   * itself, so a plain `.blog-prose h2` selector here would never match.
   * Every size/colour is a `var(--prose*)` custom property sourced from
   * tailwind.config.mjs in the frontmatter above (`define:vars`), never a
   * second hardcoded literal — CSS custom properties inherit normally down
   * the DOM regardless of Astro's own scoping mechanism, so the values
   * declared on this component's root elements reach the slotted content
   * exactly like any other inherited CSS property. */

  /* The first block of the article sits directly under the header band's own
   * bottom padding plus this section's own top padding. Its own margin on top
   * of those two is the third of the three stacked gaps the owner reported as
   * "a large awkward vertical gap" before the first paragraph. Zeroed here
   * rather than by shaving one of the paddings, because the paddings are what
   * set the band's proportions and the margin is the one that carries no
   * information at all in first position. */
  .blog-prose > :global(:first-child) {
    margin-block-start: 0;
  }

  /* `margin-block: <start> <end>`, with a deliberately large start value: the
   * space ABOVE a section heading is what tells a reader a new section has
   * begun. A heading that is merely bigger, with even space on both sides,
   * reads as an emphasised paragraph. */
  /* The table-of-contents rail's links are in-page anchors, and the site
   * header is `position: fixed` — so without this the browser scrolls the
   * targeted heading to y=0, directly underneath the header, and the reader
   * lands on the paragraph after the section they asked for.
   * `scroll-margin-block-start`, not the physical `scroll-margin-top`:
   * logical properties throughout, same rule as everywhere else here. The
   * value is the `sticky-top` token (header height plus air), the same one
   * that positions the rail itself, so the two cannot drift. */
  .blog-prose :global(h2),
  .blog-prose :global(h3) {
    scroll-margin-block-start: var(--proseAnchorOffset);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(h2) {
    margin-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.25rem) 0.75rem;
    font-family: var(--proseFontSans);
    font-weight: var(--proseWeightSemi);
    font-size: var(--proseH2Size);
    line-height: var(--proseH2Leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--proseH2Tracking);
    color: var(--proseColorPaper);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(h3) {
    margin-block: clamp(1.75rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem) 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--proseFontSans);
    font-weight: var(--proseWeightSemi);
    font-size: var(--proseH3Size);
    line-height: var(--proseH3Leading);
    letter-spacing: var(--proseH3Tracking);
    color: var(--proseColorPaper);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(p) {
    margin-block: 1.25em 0;
    font-size: var(--proseBodySize);
    line-height: var(--proseLineHeightLatin);
    color: var(--proseColorBody);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(ul),
  .blog-prose :global(ol) {
    margin-block: 1.25em 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25em;
    font-size: var(--proseBodySize);
    line-height: var(--proseLineHeightLatin);
    color: var(--proseColorBody);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(ul) {
    list-style-type: disc;
  }

  .blog-prose :global(ol) {
    list-style-type: decimal;
  }

  .blog-prose :global(li + li) {
    margin-block-start: 0.5em;
  }

  .blog-prose :global(blockquote) {
    margin-block: 1.75em;
    margin-inline: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25em;
    border-inline-start: var(--proseMarkerWidth) solid var(--proseColorAccent);
    font-size: var(--proseLedeSize);
    line-height: var(--proseLineHeightLatin);
    color: var(--proseColorPaper);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(hr) {
    margin-block: 2.5em;
    border: 0;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--proseColorHairline);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(strong) {
    font-weight: var(--proseWeightSemi);
    color: var(--proseColorPaper);
  }

  .blog-prose :global(a) {
    color: var(--proseColorAccent);
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
  }

  /* `:global([dir="rtl"])`, not a bare `[dir="rtl"]` — known-issues.md's P5
   * trap, already documented in NavDrawer.astro's own header comment:
   * Astro's per-component style scoping silently adds its own scope
   * attribute to any selector token not explicitly wrapped in `:global()`,
   * which would require `[dir="rtl"]` (set on `<html>`) to ALSO carry this
   * component's scope attribute — never true — and the whole rule would
   * silently never match. Verified against this exact bug in the built
   * output before writing this comment.
   *
   * h2/h3 ARE listed here now, unlike before this redesign. The old file's
   * comment argued they could be left out because global.css's base-layer
   * `[dir="rtl"] h2, h3 { line-height: … }` rule already covered them — true
   * only while these rules declared no line-height of their own. They now do
   * (see the frontmatter comment on why a bare `font-size` in hand-written
   * CSS brings neither of a fontSize token's companion values with it), and
   * an Astro component `<style>` block is UNLAYERED while global.css's rule
   * sits inside `@layer base`, so unlayered wins outright regardless of
   * specificity or source order — Arabic headings would have silently
   * inherited the Latin 1.25/1.35 leading. Letter-spacing is overridden for
   * the same reason. */
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(h2),
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(h3) {
    line-height: var(--proseLineHeadingArabic);
    letter-spacing: var(--proseLetterSpacingNone);
  }

  /* Paragraphs/lists/blockquotes get their own explicit RTL rule because THIS
   * file, not global.css, is what sets their (Latin) line-height in the first
   * place. */
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(p),
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(ul),
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(ol),
  :global([dir="rtl"]) .blog-prose :global(blockquote) {
    line-height: var(--proseLineHeightArabic);
    letter-spacing: var(--proseLetterSpacingNone);
  }

  /* The topics list's separator — same rule, same reasoning, as
   * BlogIndex.astro's own copy (a `<ul>` may only contain `<li>`, so the dot
   * is a pseudo-element rather than a `<span>` assistive tech would read
   * out). Duplicated rather than lifted into global.css: two components, one
   * short rule each, and a global utility for it would be a third place to
